  "probe_arm_count": 21,
  "publication_allowed": false,
  "question": "when does a causal state have to cross a stage boundary, and is the existing protocol safe and worth its handoff plus scheduling cost?",
  "route_rationale": [
    "the OpSpec workspace declares the handoff capacity in the task ABI, so the harness can reason about final_state and read_write state without trusting a candidate-owned size",
    "the correctness protocol already poisons that workspace on out1/out3 and zeroes it on out2, so a stale, partial or unpublished handoff changes the result between trials and is refused",
    "moving the same bytes to candidate scratch buys no dispatch and no traffic saving; the measured handoff cost is a property of the cut and of how many threads move the state, not of where the buffer is declared",
    "the only legal scratch shape is a two-buffer ping-pong: a single buffer that both receives and hands on the state is refused as uninitialized_poison (it is read_write), and the ping-pong moves exactly the same bytes as the declared workspace while adding one kernel argument and a different slot layout -- identity churn with no measured gain.  It is also unrunnable today: every bundle + Host Program + scratch candidate is blocked by the v2 worker geometry identity"
  ],
